TOOL_RESULT retrieve_history:
{"results": [], "n_results": 0}

Stage 2. Using the retrieval result above (follow-up tools are allowed within the tool budget), complete steps 4 to 9
and reply with the final action. Output schema (all fields required):
{
 "research_concepts": [
  "concept"
 ],
 "methodological_questions": [
  "question"
 ],
 "historical_evidence": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a record returned by a tool",
   "status_as_recorded": "outcome status exactly as stated in the record (supported | rejected | inconclusive | untestable | artifact | not a hypothesis test | no result recorded)",
   "finding": "what the record states, in its own terms"
  }
 ],
 "failed_approaches": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a returned record",
   "what_failed": "approach",
   "why": "failure mechanism or limitation as recorded"
  }
 ],
 "evidence_establishes": [
  "what the retrieved evidence establishes for this question"
 ],
 "evidence_does_not_establish": [
  "what it leaves open or cannot show"
 ],
 "own_inferences": [
  "your inference, not stated in any record"
 ],
 "uncertainties": [
  "uncertainty or missing information"
 ],
 "historical_effect": {
  "changed_decision": "yes | no | no_historical_evidence",
  "explanation": "whether and how the historical evidence changed the decision"
 },
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": "number in [0,1]: probability the question can be answered with the stated data at useful power",
  "blocking_constraints": [
   {
    "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
    "text": "string"
   }
  ]
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "yes | partly | no | unknown",
  "cited": [
   "identifiers of prior work relied on"
  ],
  "summary": "string"
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
   "text": "string"
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": "probability",
  "rejected": "probability",
  "untestable": "probability",
  "artifact": "probability",
  "uncertain": "probability"
 },
 "decision": "proceed | proceed_with_changes | do_not_proceed",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "string",
  "unit": "string",
  "sample": "string",
  "power": "string",
  "controls": "string",
  "falsification": "string",
  "multiplicity": "string",
  "decision_rule": "string",
  "data_version": "string"
 }
}
Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{
  "research_concepts": [
    "cutting (off-ball movement to the basket)",
    "driving (on-ball creation off the dribble)",
    "shaper (high-usage initiator/creator influencing offensive structure)",
    "role stability / transferability (consistency of individual player performance profile across different team contexts)",
    "structural role (the functional niche a player occupies in an offense)"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
    "How to operationally define 'cut-heavy' vs 'drive-heavy' from play-type possession records?",
    "How to quantify 'role' in a way that captures the structural influence of a shaper?",
    "What constitutes a 'travel' event: change of team, coach, system, season?",
    "How to measure 'travels better': smaller within-player role distance after a context change?",
    "What confounders (age, contract year, injury, team talent) could produce spurious differences in role stability?",
    "What sample size is required to detect a practically meaningful difference in role stability between groups?"
  ],
  "historical_evidence": [],
  "failed_approaches": [],
  "evidence_establishes": [
    "No prior evidence from this project was retrieved; the question has not been studied internally before."
  ],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "Whether cut-heavy shapers' roles are more stable than drive-heavy ones.",
    "What the typical effect size of play-style on role portability is.",
    "How to reliably classify shapers into cut-heavy vs drive-heavy from available data."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "The question implies a causal-comparative design: differing play-style (cut vs drive) is the independent variable, and role stability across contexts is the dependent variable.",
    "Operationalizing 'shaper' likely requires filtering for high-usage or high-possession-initiation players; 'cut-heavy' vs 'drive-heavy' can be defined by the proportion of initiated possessions that are cuts vs drives, using the graph database of play-type records.",
    "'Travels better' can be measured as the within-player similarity (e.g., 1 - Euclidean distance) of z-scored offensive role metrics (usage, assist rate, shot frequency by zone, turnover rate, points per possession) between consecutive seasons or pre/post trade, comparing the average similarity between groups.",
    "A major threat is regression to the mean: if a shaper is classified as extreme on a dichotomized continuous measure (cut frequency), their subsequent role stability may be inflated by measurement error, not true portability.",
    "Confounding by player quality, age, contract status, and team offensive style could easily dominate; with only ~24 units, adequate control is unlikely.",
    "The stated n=24 likely refers to the number of shapers who both changed teams during the observation window and meet inclusion criteria; this is very small for detecting a between-group difference in stability."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "The exact operational definitions of 'shaper', 'cut-heavy', 'drive-heavy', and 'role' are not yet specified and will influence testability.",
    "The number of 'travel' events (team changes) available in the data may be larger than 24 if mid-season trades are fully exploited, but the stated n=24 suggests a preliminary count.",
    "The reliability and completeness of the derived play-type possession records (initiator, play type) are unknown.",
    "Whether there is a large enough pool of cut-heavy shapers (who also change teams) for a meaningful comparison."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "no_historical_evidence",
    "explanation": "No historical evidence was retrieved, so the decision is based entirely on the current feasibility assessment."
  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.25,
    "blocking_constraints": [
      {
        "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
        "text": "n=24 is insufficient to detect a plausible between-group difference in role stability with adequate power, even for a large effect."
      },
      {
        "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
        "text": "The exposure of interest (shaper changing teams) is rare; combined with the need to split into cut-heavy and drive-heavy, cell sizes may drop below 10."
      },
      {
        "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
        "text": "Cut/drive classification from derived play-type records may be noisy, and role distance metrics have unknown reliability across team contexts."
      },
      {
        "code": "RTM_SELECTION",
        "text": "Dichotomizing a continuous play-style variable (e.g., cut%) risks selecting extreme groups that regress toward the mean in subsequent role measures, biasing the comparison."
      },
      {
        "code": "CONFOUNDING",
        "text": "Reasons for team change (trade vs free agency, injury, decline) differ systematically by play style and age, confounding the relationship with subsequent role stability."
      }
    ]
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "unknown",
    "cited": [],
    "summary": "No earlier project studies were retrieved, so it is unknown whether the question has been substantially answered previously."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
      "text": "The projected n=24 likely yields low statistical power for a between-group comparison of continuous stability scores, especially after controlling for confounders."
    },
    {
      "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
      "text": "The intersection of 'shapers who changed teams' and the cut/drive dichotomy may yield very small groups, increasing vulnerability to outliers."
    },
    {
      "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
      "text": "Reliability of the play-type classification and the derived role distance measure could be low, especially if some play types (e.g., cuts) are less frequent and more prone to misclassification."
    },
    {
      "code": "RTM_SELECTION",
      "text": "Classifying players as cut-heavy based on a single season's data exaggerates the extremity of the group, causing role stability to appear higher or lower spuriously."
    },
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "Age, contract status, injury, and team offensive system all influence both play-style classification and role stability; with small n adjustment is difficult."
    },
    {
      "code": "MULTIPLE_TESTING",
      "text": "If role stability is measured on many dimensions (scoring efficiency, playmaking, rebounding, usage), multiple comparisons will inflate false positive risk."
    },
    {
      "code": "LEAKAGE_LOOKAHEAD",
      "text": "If play-style classification and role stability use data from the same season, independence is violated; a temporal split is necessary."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.10,
    "rejected": 0.10,
    "untestable": 0.50,
    "artifact": 0.20,
    "uncertain": 0.10
  },
  "decision": "do_not_proceed",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "ATE of being a cut-heavy (vs drive-heavy) shaper on within-player role stability (Euclidean distance between standardized offensive performance vectors) across different team contexts.",
    "unit": "player-season-to-season transition where the player changed teams, for players classified as shapers.",
    "sample": "All players with usage rate > 20% and >500 total possessions initiated in the season prior to a team change, spanning 2022-23 through 2025-26 regular seasons and playoffs. Play-style classification: cut-heavy if >40% of initiated possessions are 'cut' play types; drive-heavy if >30% are 'drive' play types and <20% are 'cut' (mutually exclusive groups, with an excluded mixed group). Minimum 20 games played in both pre- and post-change seasons.",
    "power": "With an estimated n=24 (12 per group) and a plausible standardized effect size d=0.4, power for a two-sample t-test at alpha=0.05 is approximately 0.15-0.25. To achieve 0.80 power would require n≈100 per group.",
    "controls": "Age (at time of change), minutes per game in pre-change season, team offensive rating in pre-change season, injury-related games missed, whether the change was in-season (trade) or off-season, and whether the player changed conferences.",
    "falsification": "Test whether role stability in a control set of shapers who stayed with the same team shows the same cut-vs-drive difference; if the effect is identical, the travel claim is falsified. Also, check if the result holds when classification is based on career-long play-type frequencies rather than the single pre-change season.",
    "multiplicity": "Pre-register a single composite measure of role distance (e.g., Mahalanobis distance on a fixed set of core offensive metrics) and a single primary test. Any exploratory breakdowns by individual metrics will be reported with unadjusted p-values and flagged as secondary.",
    "decision_rule": "Not applicable (do_not_proceed). A minimum viable test would require a sample size of at least 60 per group and a validated, dimension-reduced measure of offensive role.",
    "data_version": "Per-game NBA stats API files and derived play-type possession graph database as of 2026-08-08."
  }
}

